Dict{"step_by_step_thinking": "The question involves understanding the concept of length-time bias and lead-time bias in the context of screening tests. The new screening test detects the cancer 2 years earlier, but the cancer has an average survival rate of 3 years after the onset of symptoms. This means that the cancer is detected before it becomes clinically significant, leading to an overestimation of survival time due to lead-time bias.

To prevent bias in the conclusions, we need to account for the fact that the cancer is detected earlier but the natural course of the disease is not altered. This can be addressed by using mortality rates instead of survival rates in the analysis. Mortality rates would give a more accurate picture of the actual impact on life expectancy, as they account for the time from diagnosis to death, rather than just the time from diagnosis to symptom onset.

